# DESCRIPTION

Between nodes, files are served in Plan 9's 9P2000, as intro(5) of Plan 9 gives it; on one node, the Node protocol serves them (node(5)).
A client sends T-messages and a server answers each with its R-message, or with Rerror, under the request's tag.
Numbers are little-endian; a string, `[s]`, is a count[2] and that many bytes of UTF-8, holding no NUL; a qid, `[13]`, is type[1], version[4] and path[8].
A stat, `stat[n]`, is a count[2] and a stat's bytes, which begin with their own size[2]:

```text
size[2] type[2] dev[4] qid[13] mode[4] atime[4] mtime[4] length[8]
name[s] uid[s] gid[s] muid[s]
```

Owners are strings, and there are no numeric ids: who may do what is decided by keys and capabilities, not by a uid.

| Type | Number |
| Tversion, Rversion | 100, 101 |
| Tauth, Rauth | 102, 103 |
| Tattach, Rattach | 104, 105 |
| Rerror | 107 (there is no Terror, 106) |
| Tflush, Rflush | 108, 109 |
| Twalk, Rwalk | 110, 111 |
| Topen, Ropen | 112, 113 |
| Tcreate, Rcreate | 114, 115 |
| Tread, Rread | 116, 117 |
| Twrite, Rwrite | 118, 119 |
| Tclunk, Rclunk | 120, 121 |
| Tremove, Rremove | 122, 123 |
| Tstat, Rstat | 124, 125 |
| Twstat, Rwstat | 126, 127 |
| Trename, Rrename (9P2000.vx) | 128, 129 |

A connection begins with Tversion, its tag `NOTAG` (65535).
Ours is `9P2000.vx`: a VectraOS server answers it, and the extensions it names are new message types, never changes to these.
A server answers `9P2000` for any other 9P2000 dialect, `9P2000.L` and `9P2000.u` among them, and `unknown` for anything else; a client that asked for `9P2000.vx` and got `9P2000` speaks plain 9P2000.
A plain listener (9gate(4)) answers `9P2000` whatever is asked: the extensions travel only between nodes, over Noise IK.

`9P2000.vx`'s messages are types from 128 up, a T-message even and its R-message the next.
A server that doesn't know one answers it with Rerror `unknown message`, and the client does without it on that connection.

Trename moves <fid>'s file into the directory <newdirfid> as <name>, replacing a file of that name there, where wstat's name only renames within a directory; <fid> then names the file where it went.
A rename between two mounts on the server is refused, `Invalid cross-device link`.

A Twstat whose fields are all "don't touch" (~0, or empty strings) asks the server to make the file durable, as Plan 9's convention is: on a node, fs(4)'s sync.
The msize is the smaller of the two sides', at least 256; a read or write carries at most <msize> − 24 bytes.

A message is refused when its bytes are not exactly one message: a size that is not its own, a field that doesn't fit, a byte past its last field, more than 16 names or qids in a walk, an unknown type other than a `9P2000.vx` T-message, or a string holding a NUL or bytes that are not UTF-8.

